<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Smith (explorer)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1580">1580</a>–<a href="/years/1631">1631</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Captain John Smith</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Smith was an English soldier and explorer who helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> in 1607 and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> colony as president of its council in 1608 and 1609. His leadership kept the struggling settlement alive through its early hardships. His writings, including the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pocahontas">Pocahontas</a>, and his maps of Virginia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> shaped how the colonies were seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-smith-explorer.jpg" alt="John Smith (explorer)" width="320" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Book by Capt. John Smith, engraver uncertain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houghton_STC_22790_-_Generall_Historie_of_Virginia,_New_England,_and_the_Summer_Isles,_John_Smith.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Virginia Company founded Jamestown in May 1607, Wahunsenacah first hoped to make the English his tributaries; in December 1607 a hunting party under Opechancanough captured <b>John Smith</b> and brought him to Werowocomoco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/powhatan-confederacy">Powhatan Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>John Smith</b> met them in 1608 on Chesapeake Bay he described them as tall and well armed.</blockquote><a href="/state/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Willoughby, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.22,0.21&amp;year=1613" aria-label="Willoughby on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4555 332 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Smith (explorer)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5005 602h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5005 602h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.22,0.21&amp;year=1613">What was here in 1613? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
